# {{PACK_NAME}}

A sample Kindgi pack in Python — scaffolded by `kindgi init --template=python`.

Two tools (`echo`, `greet`), one guardrail (`response-not-empty`), one agent
that uses both tools and the guardrail, and one flow that runs the echo tool.

## Layout

```
{{PACK_NAME}}/
├── pyproject.toml                     # [tool.kindgi] — the pack's id and version
├── tools/echo.py                      # @tool — pydantic in, pydantic out
├── tools/greet.py                     # @tool
├── guardrails/response_not_empty.py   # @guardrail — a zero-LLM check over the run
├── agents/echo_agent.py               # Agent(...) — data
├── flows/echo_flow.py                 # Flow(...) — data
└── tests/test_tools.py                # the tools and the check, called directly
```

## Run

```sh
uv sync          # .venv with kindgi and the dev tools
uv run pytest
kindgi dev       # boots Kindgi locally and runs this pack's code, reloading on save
```

`kindgi dev` uses the pack's `.venv/bin/python` (or `dev.python` in
`[tool.kindgi]`). Then, from another terminal:

```sh
kindgi runs start --agent={{PACK_ID}}.echo-agent --input='{"userMessage":"hi"}'
```

## Use a real model

`kindgi dev` gives a new pack `dev-echo`, a fallback provider that isn't a
model: it calls the first tool and replies "Tool responded: …", after a
warning line that says so. It answers only while no other provider fits. To
use a real model, set one LLM provider's key and register its preset, with
`kindgi dev` running, from another terminal. For Claude:

```sh
kindgi secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY --env=local --scope=tenant   # no-echo prompt; writes .env.local
kindgi providers register --preset=anthropic                      # Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5 (default), Haiku 5.5, Haiku 4.5
```

Claude takes over from dev-echo on the next turn; nothing to restart.
`--models=claude-sonnet-5-5` registers one model only. OpenAI
(`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `--preset=openai`), Gemini (`GEMINI_API_KEY`,
`--preset=gemini-api`), Groq (`GROQ_API_KEY`, `--preset=groq`) and
OpenRouter, a hosted service your prompts pass through (`OPENROUTER_API_KEY`,
`--preset=openrouter`), work the same way; `kindgi providers presets` lists
the presets (Gemini on Vertex AI too). Picking a model per
agent, and other providers: `.claude/skills/kindgi-authoring-providers/SKILL.md`.

## Build

`kindgi build --env=<name>` makes the pack's image: dependencies from `uv.lock`
(run `uv lock` first), the index built in the image, the pack service as its
entrypoint.
